1
00:00:00,980 --> 00:00:06,340
The day after I kicked Pouri where it hurts, they exiled me to Dezashib.

2
00:00:06,900 --> 00:00:10,900
It was my first taste of the bitterness of separation.

3
00:00:11,180 --> 00:00:18,900
After 21 days, when school reopened, I was pardoned and rushed back to see Leili.

4
00:00:19,700 --> 00:00:26,260
But seeing Pouri drive Leili to school every day dashed all my hopes.

5
00:00:27,600 --> 00:00:31,760
While I was in exile, Pouri had had an operation in hospital,

6
00:00:31,800 --> 00:00:35,740
and they'd removed one of his pair.

7
00:00:36,860 --> 00:00:39,840
Uncle Colonel, deeply worried about Pouri's condition,

8
00:00:39,880 --> 00:00:46,760
had secretly arranged for Akhtar, the Aspiran's sister, to test Pouri.

9
00:00:47,660 --> 00:00:51,550
Passing this test was the condition for Pouri marrying Leili.

10
00:00:52,480 --> 00:00:54,800
To save my love, I decided

11
00:00:54,840 --> 00:00:58,405
to wreck that test at any cost.

12
00:03:13,320 --> 00:03:15,430
Go in. Everything's set.

13
00:03:15,480 --> 00:03:17,230
Only the wine's in the basement.

14
00:03:17,280 --> 00:03:18,505
Okay.

15
00:03:59,240 --> 00:04:01,960
Mash Ghasem! Mash Ghasem! Where are you going?

16
00:04:02,900 --> 00:04:03,870
Where are you, son?

17
00:04:03,920 --> 00:04:05,210
Here.

18
00:04:05,960 --> 00:04:07,600
Come. Come.

19
00:04:12,120 --> 00:04:13,850
Son, we came to give you the news.

20
00:04:13,900 --> 00:04:17,470
From inside our room we were keeping an eye on the Colonel's house.

21
00:04:17,520 --> 00:04:19,500
All of a sudden that hussy showed up.

22
00:04:19,540 --> 00:04:20,540
All dolled up,

23
00:04:20,580 --> 00:04:21,800
wearing a chador too.

24
00:04:21,840 --> 00:04:23,900
She came into the garden, we kept watching,

25
00:04:23,940 --> 00:04:25,300
we saw it with our own eyes,

26
00:04:25,340 --> 00:04:27,060
she went toward the Colonel's house.

27
00:04:27,100 --> 00:04:28,080
Is Pouri home too?

28
00:04:28,120 --> 00:04:29,100
Why should I lie?

29
00:04:29,140 --> 00:04:30,960
We haven't yet seen with our own eyes

30
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:32,000
him leave the house.

31
00:04:32,040 --> 00:04:34,100
Mash Ghasem, go to Uncle's house, quick.

32
00:04:34,140 --> 00:04:35,220
Get Asdollah over here,

33
00:04:35,260 --> 00:04:37,260
so if there's a ruckus he'll look out for me.

34
00:04:37,300 --> 00:04:38,100
Go.

35
00:04:38,140 --> 00:04:39,350
Yes, son.

36
00:04:39,400 --> 00:04:42,180
Be very careful the Colonel doesn't find out, now.

37
00:04:42,220 --> 00:04:44,600
If he finds out, it's your own neck.

38
00:04:44,640 --> 00:04:45,490
All right.

39
00:04:45,540 --> 00:04:47,440
Look, Mash Ghasem, nobody must know.

40
00:04:47,480 --> 00:04:49,225
Yes, son.

41
00:04:53,160 --> 00:04:57,160
I'd be so grateful, Pouri dear, if you fixed this for me.

42
00:04:57,720 --> 00:05:01,110
What about last year? Did they tax him this much last year too?

43
00:05:01,160 --> 00:05:03,740
No, last year we knew somebody.

44
00:05:03,780 --> 00:05:06,305
He pulled some strings for him.

45
00:05:07,480 --> 00:05:14,760
Well, come to the office tomorrow or the day after, I'll see what can be done.

46
00:05:15,000 --> 00:05:17,265
I need to look at his file.

47
00:05:17,600 --> 00:05:19,880
It's so hot today.

48
00:05:20,600 --> 00:05:25,470
Would you like a glass of cold sour-cherry sherbet?

49
00:05:25,520 --> 00:05:29,550
No. I'd love a glass of cold beer.

50
00:05:29,600 --> 00:05:33,150
Or some of that wine your Agha Joon gave us that time.

51
00:05:33,200 --> 00:05:36,440
Agha Joon keeps his wine in the storeroom.

52
00:05:36,480 --> 00:05:39,440
He locks... locks the door.

53
00:05:39,480 --> 00:05:41,440
Well, that's because of you.

54
00:05:41,480 --> 00:05:43,440
No, I swear, no.

55
00:05:43,480 --> 00:05:45,440
I don't drink at all.

56
00:05:45,480 --> 00:05:47,440
Oh, sure you don't.

57
00:05:47,480 --> 00:05:49,440
You say it and I believe it.

58
00:05:49,480 --> 00:05:52,440
When you take girls out to cafés and such,

59
00:05:52,480 --> 00:05:55,150
you sit there eating faloodeh, do you?

60
00:05:55,200 --> 00:05:57,070
I don't do that sort of thing.

61
00:05:57,120 --> 00:05:59,440
Are you trying to get on my nerves?

62
00:05:59,480 --> 00:06:02,440
All right, all right, stop telling fibs.

63
00:06:02,480 --> 00:06:06,980
A tall, handsome young man like you, not doing such things?

64
00:06:07,616 --> 00:06:11,360
Even if you didn't want to, girls wouldn't leave you alone.

65
00:06:12,676 --> 00:06:15,136
Go on, you rascal, take a look.

66
00:06:15,556 --> 00:06:18,060
Maybe you'll find some of your Agha Joon's wine.

67
00:06:18,100 --> 00:06:20,200
I just know they don't keep it all locked up.

68
00:06:20,240 --> 00:06:22,635
If you knew how much I'm craving it...

69
00:06:23,716 --> 00:06:26,940
Now go look. Do you want to break my heart?

70
00:06:26,980 --> 00:06:29,660
Then turn your face the other way.

71
00:06:30,916 --> 00:06:32,661
Is this all right?

72
00:06:33,116 --> 00:06:35,866
I'm crazy about shy young men.

73
00:06:35,916 --> 00:06:37,666
But don't look, okay?

74
00:06:37,716 --> 00:06:39,316
No, darling.

75
00:06:39,496 --> 00:06:40,591
Okay.

76
00:08:02,016 --> 00:08:03,966
You're so lucky.

77
00:08:04,016 --> 00:08:06,736
There was a bottle of wine in the basement.

78
00:08:07,316 --> 00:08:08,180
See, I told you.

79
00:08:08,220 --> 00:08:09,180
Wait, I'll get glasses.

80
00:08:09,220 --> 00:08:11,290
No, there's a glass here.

81
00:08:31,220 --> 00:08:33,220
Don't pour me any, okay?

82
00:08:34,460 --> 00:08:35,620
Why?

83
00:08:37,116 --> 00:08:40,220
I told you I don't drink at all.

84
00:08:49,380 --> 00:08:52,380
Mmm! Divine.

85
00:08:53,020 --> 00:08:56,010
It breaks my heart you won't drink this. Just a sip.

86
00:08:56,060 --> 00:08:58,980
On my life, you don't know how good this wine is.

87
00:08:59,076 --> 00:09:00,686
I've tasted it before.

88
00:09:00,736 --> 00:09:04,220
Cross my heart, if it's bad, don't drink it.

89
00:09:04,836 --> 00:09:06,100
But it's not good for me.

90
00:09:06,140 --> 00:09:08,680
For Akhtar's sake, cross my heart.

91
00:09:10,036 --> 00:09:11,300
It gives me a headache.

92
00:09:11,340 --> 00:09:13,680
I said drink, for my sake!

93
00:09:13,720 --> 00:09:15,040
It tastes awful.

94
00:09:15,080 --> 00:09:19,680
Please, it's wine, not poison. Have a gulp.

95
00:09:19,720 --> 00:09:21,465
I'd die for you. Drink.

96
00:09:28,980 --> 00:09:32,090
Oh, my God! You spilled it on my dress!

97
00:09:33,136 --> 00:09:34,026
It's your own fault.

98
00:09:34,076 --> 00:09:36,926
What are you doing, Pouri dear? Gently.

99
00:09:37,836 --> 00:09:38,940
Let me wipe it.

100
00:09:38,980 --> 00:09:41,560
No, not like that, it'll stain.

101
00:09:42,396 --> 00:09:44,660
Let me straighten it out later.

102
00:09:52,356 --> 00:09:54,260
Ooh, you sly thing.

103
00:09:54,876 --> 00:09:56,686
Shall I go tell everyone

104
00:09:56,776 --> 00:09:59,110
Pouri dear stained my skirt?

105
00:09:59,160 --> 00:10:02,595
Good thing Agha Joon wasn't here, or it'd be a scandal.

106
00:10:02,916 --> 00:10:07,120
You sly fox, you're good at playing dumb.

107
00:10:07,160 --> 00:10:11,160
Well, your Agha Joon wasn't here, so I got you alone, pretty boy.

108
00:10:13,916 --> 00:10:16,086
What mosquitoes you have here.

109
00:10:16,136 --> 00:10:18,110
They bite like crazy.

110
00:10:18,160 --> 00:10:20,160
My knee's on fire.

111
00:10:21,160 --> 00:10:23,120
Look how swollen it is.

112
00:10:23,160 --> 00:10:25,120
Put your hand on it.

113
00:10:25,160 --> 00:10:27,120
Put it there.

114
00:10:27,160 --> 00:10:29,160
Rub it.

115
00:10:30,016 --> 00:10:31,761
Got any alcohol in the house,

116
00:10:31,816 --> 00:10:33,120
cologne,

117
00:10:33,160 --> 00:10:34,126
anything?

118
00:10:34,176 --> 00:10:37,160
Sure! We have alcohol. I'll go get it.

119
00:10:50,936 --> 00:10:52,161
Alcohol.

120
00:10:58,200 --> 00:11:00,316
Ah, alcohol.

121
00:11:01,160 --> 00:11:03,880
Ow, ow, ow, it stings so much!

122
00:11:07,500 --> 00:11:08,250
Easy.

123
00:11:08,296 --> 00:11:09,266
Ooh, rub a bit more.

124
00:11:09,316 --> 00:11:11,646
Hold on, it'll feel better now.

125
00:11:11,916 --> 00:11:13,120
Ooh, you devil.

126
00:11:13,160 --> 00:11:15,120
What naughty hands you have.

127
00:11:15,160 --> 00:11:17,120
And you were the one saying you don't know

128
00:11:17,160 --> 00:11:19,120
how to chase girls.

129
00:11:19,160 --> 00:11:21,120
Don't do that, Miss Akhtar.

130
00:11:21,160 --> 00:11:23,120
If my parents come back, there'll be trouble.

131
00:11:23,160 --> 00:11:25,120
Forget them, they won't be back for ages.

132
00:11:25,160 --> 00:11:27,120
Our servant might come.

133
00:11:27,160 --> 00:11:29,160
Come here, nobody's coming.

134
00:12:19,826 --> 00:12:21,831
Please don't do that.

135
00:12:24,200 --> 00:12:27,310
It's war, it's war! Like they dropped a bomb!

136
00:12:28,046 --> 00:12:29,500
What is it? What happened?

137
00:12:29,550 --> 00:12:32,270
War! War! I'm dead! Cannon fire! Oh no!

138
00:12:37,586 --> 00:12:39,200
War! Cannons!

139
00:12:39,246 --> 00:12:40,680
Oh, I'm dead.

140
00:12:40,726 --> 00:12:42,406
They dropped a bomb.

141
00:12:42,806 --> 00:12:43,495
It's war!

142
00:12:43,545 --> 00:12:47,000
I'm dead. Save me!

143
00:12:47,366 --> 00:12:48,110
I'm dead.

144
00:12:48,160 --> 00:12:50,120
They dropped a bomb.

145
00:12:50,160 --> 00:12:50,960
You?

146
00:12:51,000 --> 00:12:51,680
You? So it was you?

147
00:12:51,720 --> 00:12:52,280
No, Pouri, really!

148
00:12:52,320 --> 00:12:53,266
No, on my father's life!

149
00:12:53,306 --> 00:12:55,120
You scoundrel, I'll kill you!

150
00:12:55,160 --> 00:12:56,120
Dear Uncle can die if it was me!

151
00:12:56,160 --> 00:12:57,160
Your father can die!

152
00:12:57,200 --> 00:12:59,950
Don't curse!

153
00:13:00,270 --> 00:13:01,656
What was that noise?

154
00:13:01,706 --> 00:13:03,636
This bastard threw a bomb into our house.

155
00:13:03,686 --> 00:13:06,176
You little beggar! Tell me why you did it!

156
00:13:06,226 --> 00:13:07,711
It wasn't me.

157
00:13:10,406 --> 00:13:12,801
This time you'll get it good.

158
00:13:17,066 --> 00:13:18,936
You low-born bastard!

159
00:13:18,986 --> 00:13:20,816
Dear Uncle, may I bury Dear Uncle if I'm lying!

160
00:13:20,866 --> 00:13:21,656
When I heard it...

161
00:13:21,706 --> 00:13:23,256
I let you off before, now you're cocky.

162
00:13:23,306 --> 00:13:25,676
I'll cane the soles of your feet till...

163
00:13:25,726 --> 00:13:27,466
It wasn't me.

164
00:13:27,666 --> 00:13:31,356
I'll sue you! I'll sue you and your bastard father!

165
00:13:31,406 --> 00:13:34,191
I'll throw you both behind bars.

166
00:13:37,686 --> 00:13:39,496
Take his shoes off.

167
00:13:39,606 --> 00:13:40,440
Bastard!

168
00:13:40,480 --> 00:13:42,160
I'll teach you!

169
00:13:42,366 --> 00:13:43,180
Let go of my foot!

170
00:13:43,220 --> 00:13:44,720
Let go or I'll kick again!

171
00:13:44,760 --> 00:13:47,140
The little bastard talks back, too!

172
00:13:47,180 --> 00:13:50,020
Why are you beating the boy?

173
00:13:50,060 --> 00:13:51,260
Brother, this boy,

174
00:13:51,300 --> 00:13:54,060
this rotten bastard, tried to kill Pouri.

175
00:13:54,100 --> 00:13:54,720
Yes.

176
00:13:54,760 --> 00:13:57,420
Have you lost your minds?

177
00:13:58,566 --> 00:14:00,800
At such a dangerous moment,

178
00:14:00,840 --> 00:14:04,080
instead of reinforcing our defenses,

179
00:14:04,120 --> 00:14:07,200
you gang up on this boy and beat him?

180
00:14:07,240 --> 00:14:10,200
Uncle, this scum threw a grenade into our house!

181
00:14:10,240 --> 00:14:11,580
Silence!

182
00:14:12,226 --> 00:14:14,231
Stupidity has its limits!

183
00:14:14,700 --> 00:14:18,430
We've wasted so much time that the real assassin

184
00:14:18,480 --> 00:14:20,875
is thirty miles away by now.

185
00:14:23,126 --> 00:14:24,600
The bastards!

186
00:14:24,640 --> 00:14:26,800
Either they throw bombs from the alley

187
00:14:26,840 --> 00:14:28,700
or from an airplane.

188
00:14:29,906 --> 00:14:35,960
Just a few minutes ago I heard their balloon up in the sky myself.

189
00:14:36,806 --> 00:14:40,140
May God bring ruin on these English!

190
00:14:40,180 --> 00:14:41,360
The English again?

191
00:14:41,400 --> 00:14:43,100
Starting again, Brother?

192
00:14:43,140 --> 00:14:45,240
Yes, the English again!

193
00:14:45,280 --> 00:14:46,880
The English again!

194
00:14:46,920 --> 00:14:50,640
You think the English hatred of me

195
00:14:51,646 --> 00:14:53,180
is all in my head?

196
00:14:53,246 --> 00:14:53,896
No.

197
00:14:53,946 --> 00:14:57,146
So I'm talking nonsense, am I?

198
00:14:57,626 --> 00:15:01,640
You people are the death of me!

199
00:15:01,680 --> 00:15:05,200
I don't sleep day or night

200
00:15:05,240 --> 00:15:09,740
to protect you from the English.

201
00:15:09,780 --> 00:15:16,040
I'm gambling my life, my honor, my good name.

202
00:15:16,726 --> 00:15:19,140
What better proof do you want?

203
00:15:20,466 --> 00:15:22,960
Can't you see

204
00:15:23,586 --> 00:15:25,300
they're, from the alley,

205
00:15:26,046 --> 00:15:27,820
from an airplane,

206
00:15:27,860 --> 00:15:29,100
or, I don't know,

207
00:15:29,140 --> 00:15:31,400
from their fathers' graves,

208
00:15:31,440 --> 00:15:34,660
raining bombs on my house?

209
00:15:35,266 --> 00:15:39,020
How do I get it into your thick heads, you fools?

210
00:15:39,966 --> 00:15:43,060
You're the death of me!

211
00:15:43,666 --> 00:15:48,780
God, deliver me from these relatives!

212
00:15:48,820 --> 00:15:50,376
God, let me die!

213
00:15:50,416 --> 00:15:51,466
God help me!

214
00:15:51,506 --> 00:15:53,520
Brother, what's wrong?

215
00:15:53,560 --> 00:15:54,360
Dear Uncle!

216
00:15:54,400 --> 00:15:55,160
Brother!

217
00:15:55,200 --> 00:15:56,496
Dear Uncle!

218
00:15:56,546 --> 00:15:57,960
You want to kill Dear Uncle!

219
00:15:58,000 --> 00:15:58,840
I know it!

220
00:15:58,880 --> 00:16:01,200
It's this fatherless boy's fault!

221
00:16:01,240 --> 00:16:02,520
Beggar! Son of a dog!

222
00:16:02,560 --> 00:16:03,600
Pouri, don't let him escape!

223
00:16:03,640 --> 00:16:04,540
I'll kill him!

224
00:16:04,580 --> 00:16:05,640
If you come any closer,

225
00:16:05,680 --> 00:16:07,000
I'll kill you both!

226
00:16:07,040 --> 00:16:08,020
You're murderers!

227
00:16:08,060 --> 00:16:08,900
Instead of...

228
00:16:08,940 --> 00:16:10,420
You killed Dear Uncle!

229
00:16:10,460 --> 00:16:11,860
I'll kill you both!

230
00:16:11,900 --> 00:16:13,726
Saeid dear, what are you doing?

231
00:16:13,766 --> 00:16:14,816
Get out of the way, man.

232
00:16:14,866 --> 00:16:16,786
Have you gone mad, boy?

233
00:16:16,826 --> 00:16:19,156
You killed Dear Uncle!

234
00:16:21,820 --> 00:16:22,980
Saeid!

235
00:16:23,820 --> 00:16:24,770
Saeid, what are you doing?

236
00:16:24,820 --> 00:16:26,780
He wants to kill me!

237
00:16:26,820 --> 00:16:27,780
Are you crazy?

238
00:16:27,820 --> 00:16:28,770
Hunting farm animals?

239
00:16:28,820 --> 00:16:30,780
Uncle Asdollah, they killed Dear Uncle!

240
00:16:30,820 --> 00:16:32,380
I want revenge!

241
00:16:32,430 --> 00:16:33,360
Enough. What happened?

242
00:16:33,400 --> 00:16:34,600
Who tried to kill Sir?

243
00:16:34,650 --> 00:16:35,780
Let me kill them, Uncle!

244
00:16:35,820 --> 00:16:37,780
Let them off. This Pouri's a dumb ass.

245
00:16:37,820 --> 00:16:39,820
What's wrong with Sir?

246
00:16:39,870 --> 00:16:40,696
He wants to kill!

247
00:16:40,746 --> 00:16:41,841
Sir!

248
00:16:42,826 --> 00:16:46,826
And instead of fetching a doctor, these two took cover.

249
00:16:47,346 --> 00:16:48,520
Come on out, Colonel.

250
00:16:48,560 --> 00:16:49,560
I'm scared.

251
00:16:49,600 --> 00:16:51,300
I beg you,

252
00:16:51,340 --> 00:16:53,840
Uncle Asdollah, he wants to kill us!

253
00:16:53,880 --> 00:16:55,240
Uncle Asdollah!

254
00:16:55,280 --> 00:16:57,780
Promise this kid won't touch the revolver?

255
00:16:57,820 --> 00:17:00,376
Get up and come out. Shame on you!

256
00:17:00,426 --> 00:17:01,100
Colonel, move it!

257
00:17:01,140 --> 00:17:02,300
Sir's in bad shape.

258
00:17:02,340 --> 00:17:04,560
Pouri, run and get Dr. Naser-ol-Hokama!

259
00:17:04,600 --> 00:17:05,400
Run—

260
00:17:05,440 --> 00:17:07,400
Run, go on!

261
00:17:07,440 --> 00:17:10,680
Sir, Sir, what happened all of a sudden?

262
00:17:10,720 --> 00:17:12,140
Sir! Sir!

263
00:17:13,426 --> 00:17:17,130
Won't anyone tell me what happened? Why has Sir fainted?

264
00:17:17,180 --> 00:17:18,936
Bring me that blanket.

265
00:17:18,986 --> 00:17:22,280
Poor man's lying here, and they've raised hell.

266
00:17:22,320 --> 00:17:24,560
Here, quick, quick, bring a syringe.

267
00:17:24,600 --> 00:17:26,020
He's skin and bones.

268
00:17:26,060 --> 00:17:27,860
It's all that bastard's fault!

269
00:17:27,900 --> 00:17:29,680
He threw a bomb into our house!

270
00:17:29,720 --> 00:17:31,520
It made a bang you wouldn't believe!

271
00:17:31,560 --> 00:17:32,920
He wanted to kill dear Pouri!

272
00:17:32,960 --> 00:17:34,720
This bastard, God knows

273
00:17:34,760 --> 00:17:37,040
what he wanted to do to dear Pouri.

274
00:17:37,080 --> 00:17:38,720
Dear Pouri was fine.

275
00:17:38,760 --> 00:17:39,680
Did it hit Sir?

276
00:17:39,720 --> 00:17:42,600
No, it broke the windowpane.

277
00:17:42,640 --> 00:17:44,760
Huh, Sir fainted at the sound of breaking glass?

278
00:17:44,800 --> 00:17:45,700
No, no.

279
00:17:45,740 --> 00:17:48,520
No, Brother came later.

280
00:17:49,206 --> 00:17:50,800
I said this bastard

281
00:17:50,840 --> 00:17:53,080
threw a grenade to kill Pouri.

282
00:17:53,120 --> 00:17:55,400
Brother said it was the English.

283
00:17:55,440 --> 00:17:56,800
Whether it was him

284
00:17:56,840 --> 00:17:58,580
or the English, why faint over it?

285
00:17:58,620 --> 00:17:59,940
Dear Uncle got really angry.

286
00:17:59,980 --> 00:18:01,380
He fought with Pouri and his father.

287
00:18:01,420 --> 00:18:03,380
Because the two of them were beating me.

288
00:18:03,420 --> 00:18:05,080
They'd tied my feet up for a caning

289
00:18:05,120 --> 00:18:06,660
beating me when Dear Uncle came.

290
00:18:06,700 --> 00:18:09,280
It may well have been the English.

291
00:18:09,320 --> 00:18:11,380
Where would this innocent child get a bomb?

292
00:18:11,420 --> 00:18:14,640
Don't let that innocent face fool you.

293
00:18:14,680 --> 00:18:17,320
He's a little bastard like you wouldn't believe.

294
00:18:17,360 --> 00:18:19,500
Isn't he the one who kicked him that time?

295
00:18:19,550 --> 00:18:20,310
Uncle Asdollah,

296
00:18:20,360 --> 00:18:21,320
I know nothing about it.

297
00:18:21,360 --> 00:18:23,320
You can ask Akhtar.

298
00:18:23,360 --> 00:18:24,585
Moment!

299
00:18:25,290 --> 00:18:27,320
Mr. Colonel, moment!

300
00:18:27,360 --> 00:18:29,410
Moment!

301
00:18:29,530 --> 00:18:31,320
What was Miss Akhtar doing here?

302
00:18:31,360 --> 00:18:33,320
Uncle Asdollah, I swear, when it went off,

303
00:18:33,360 --> 00:18:37,320
I saw with my own eyes Miss Akhtar run out the basement door into the alley.

304
00:18:37,360 --> 00:18:38,320
Moment!

305
00:18:38,360 --> 00:18:40,320
What's this business with Akhtar?

306
00:18:40,360 --> 00:18:42,360
Moment!

307
00:18:44,450 --> 00:18:50,180
Akhtar, the Aspiran's sister, came to ask Pouri to help one of her relatives.

308
00:18:50,220 --> 00:18:53,360
I gather they want to tax him heavily.

309
00:18:57,550 --> 00:18:59,040
If I were you,

310
00:18:59,080 --> 00:19:02,400
I wouldn't leave that woman alone with dear Pouri.

311
00:19:02,440 --> 00:19:06,480
She uses things like that as an excuse to lead young men astray.

312
00:19:06,520 --> 00:19:09,780
If Asghar Diesel learns Akhtar came to Pouri, he'll rip his belly open.

313
00:19:09,820 --> 00:19:10,980
These toughs are touchy about their honor.

314
00:19:11,020 --> 00:19:13,020
Uncle, don't say that anywhere!

315
00:19:13,060 --> 00:19:16,020
It could reach that vile thug's ears.

316
00:19:16,060 --> 00:19:17,640
He might think that we...

317
00:19:17,680 --> 00:19:18,840
Idiot!

318
00:19:19,900 --> 00:19:20,760
What is it?

319
00:19:20,800 --> 00:19:22,160
Who told this fool to—

320
00:19:22,200 --> 00:19:23,860
Go away, man, stop raising dust!

321
00:19:23,900 --> 00:19:24,660
Go!

322
00:19:24,700 --> 00:19:25,720
I didn't catch that.

323
00:19:25,760 --> 00:19:28,550
Somebody take the broom from this dolt!

324
00:19:28,600 --> 00:19:31,905
Right, he'll go take the broom from him now.

325
00:19:34,400 --> 00:19:37,150
Soleiman, didn't you hear Dear Uncle say don't sweep?

326
00:19:37,200 --> 00:19:38,850
One says sweep,

327
00:19:38,900 --> 00:19:39,850
one says don't.

328
00:19:39,900 --> 00:19:41,530
Now Dear Uncle says don't sweep.

329
00:19:41,580 --> 00:19:43,230
Dear Uncle says don't sweep,

330
00:19:43,280 --> 00:19:44,800
Agha Jan says sweep.

331
00:19:44,850 --> 00:19:46,670
What a circus!

332
00:19:47,200 --> 00:19:49,200
Somebody go

333
00:19:49,620 --> 00:19:54,320
and fetch Aspiran Ghias Abadi.

334
00:19:54,400 --> 00:19:56,690
Brother, what do you want with the Aspiran?

335
00:19:56,740 --> 00:19:59,660
We're convinced too that the boy wasn't guilty.

336
00:19:59,700 --> 00:20:01,660
You should forgive him as well.

337
00:20:01,700 --> 00:20:08,700
I want his opinion as an expert.

338
00:20:09,260 --> 00:20:10,780
On what?

339
00:20:10,820 --> 00:20:11,900
The bomb.

340
00:20:11,940 --> 00:20:15,880
Why the Aspiran? I know far more about it than he does.

341
00:20:15,920 --> 00:20:18,760
No, don't talk nonsense.

342
00:20:18,800 --> 00:20:24,420
You wasted your whole life behind a records desk.

343
00:20:24,460 --> 00:20:29,920
You know nothing about bombs and such, Colonel.

344
00:20:29,960 --> 00:20:32,560
Sir himself knows these things better than anyone.

345
00:20:32,600 --> 00:20:35,520
Sir grew up among cannons, rifles and gunpowder.

346
00:20:35,560 --> 00:20:38,360
Even the English, if something got stuck in their cannon,

347
00:20:38,400 --> 00:20:39,660
would send for Sir.

348
00:20:39,700 --> 00:20:41,760
Enough! Shame on you!

349
00:20:41,800 --> 00:20:44,420
Even nonsense has its limits, Asdollah.

350
00:20:44,460 --> 00:20:47,560
So you're saying Sir doesn't know how to fire a cannon?

351
00:20:47,600 --> 00:20:49,520
That Sir's wars with the English

352
00:20:49,560 --> 00:20:51,520
back in the day were just hot air?

353
00:20:51,560 --> 00:20:53,890
Huh? That's what you mean, yes?

354
00:20:54,630 --> 00:20:56,780
When did I say any such thing?

355
00:20:56,820 --> 00:21:00,600
I meant this is no time for that.

356
00:21:01,730 --> 00:21:03,980
Has he come? Has Mr. Ghias Abadi come?

357
00:21:04,020 --> 00:21:05,580
Greetings to you, Sir.

358
00:21:05,620 --> 00:21:06,980
God forbid anything's wrong.

359
00:21:07,020 --> 00:21:08,520
Has something happened?

360
00:21:08,560 --> 00:21:11,560
Come in, Mr. Aspiran, come in.

361
00:21:12,080 --> 00:21:17,960
Today an explosion took place in this house.

362
00:21:19,470 --> 00:21:24,400
No doubt you came here because of the blast.

363
00:21:24,440 --> 00:21:27,900
No, Sir, we haven't heard a thing.

364
00:21:29,970 --> 00:21:32,800
Strange we didn't hear anything.

365
00:21:32,840 --> 00:21:35,600
We'd taken a little nap before noon.

366
00:21:35,640 --> 00:21:38,160
Then Qamar got to missing you,

367
00:21:38,200 --> 00:21:41,360
so we said, let's go pay our respects to Sir.

368
00:21:41,400 --> 00:21:46,000
In any case, I want you,

369
00:21:46,630 --> 00:21:51,200
as a bomb expert,

370
00:21:52,630 --> 00:21:58,660
to examine the blast site carefully

371
00:21:58,700 --> 00:22:08,580
and report to me what explosive this bomb was made with.

372
00:22:08,620 --> 00:22:11,060
Damn these bastard English!

373
00:22:11,100 --> 00:22:12,260
Silence!

374
00:22:12,470 --> 00:22:15,905
Nobody has the right to interfere in my investigation.

375
00:22:18,870 --> 00:22:21,590
Qamar, quick, fast, swift, take the baby!

376
00:22:45,070 --> 00:22:47,070
How long is this farce going to go on?

377
00:22:47,110 --> 00:22:47,680
Silence!

378
00:22:47,720 --> 00:22:49,100
Sir, let us work.

379
00:22:49,140 --> 00:22:52,380
I said no talking during the investigation.

380
00:22:52,640 --> 00:22:54,255
Get lost.

381
00:22:59,480 --> 00:23:01,700
Sir, we've cracked it.

382
00:23:01,740 --> 00:23:05,560
This bomb is the type of bomb they call a gree-nade.

383
00:23:05,600 --> 00:23:07,500
This thing you mention,

384
00:23:07,780 --> 00:23:10,140
where was it made?

385
00:23:10,180 --> 00:23:11,760
It's... English.

386
00:23:11,800 --> 00:23:14,860
Either it's Belgian-made or English.

387
00:23:14,900 --> 00:23:18,500
Back then the English made lots of these bombs.

388
00:23:18,540 --> 00:23:22,540
Now do you see? Now do you understand?

389
00:23:23,380 --> 00:23:29,540
Now, to those who believe I understand nothing,

390
00:23:30,120 --> 00:23:34,320
is it proven, or do you still have doubts?

391
00:23:35,090 --> 00:23:42,400
As Napoleon said, the only thing with no limit is stupidity.

392
00:23:42,440 --> 00:23:43,880
Stupidity!

393
00:23:44,850 --> 00:23:47,080
Sir, don't get so worked up, it's bad for you.

394
00:23:47,120 --> 00:23:50,460
When I, poor wretch, say it,

395
00:23:51,350 --> 00:23:52,900
shout it,

396
00:23:53,550 --> 00:23:55,560
roar it,

397
00:23:55,600 --> 00:23:59,800
nobody listens.

398
00:23:59,840 --> 00:24:03,260
For months now the English

399
00:24:03,870 --> 00:24:06,640
have occupied this country.

400
00:24:06,680 --> 00:24:11,520
But nobody wants to face the truth.

401
00:24:11,560 --> 00:24:14,900
But the English

402
00:24:14,940 --> 00:24:18,380
will never get their hands on me.

403
00:24:18,460 --> 00:24:22,420
I'll crush them!

404
00:24:22,660 --> 00:24:28,340
I'll set the English on fire!

405
00:24:28,580 --> 00:24:35,820
Let them throw grenades into my house, then!

406
00:24:37,860 --> 00:24:38,700
In there, Doctor.

407
00:24:38,740 --> 00:24:41,050
Huh, what's he doing in the basement?

408
00:24:41,100 --> 00:24:43,020
Doctor, will he be all right?

409
00:24:43,060 --> 00:24:46,180
Bless you, I haven't even seen him yet.

410
00:24:46,220 --> 00:24:47,860
What a question.

411
00:24:47,900 --> 00:24:49,484
Bless you.

412
00:24:49,534 --> 00:24:51,044
Ah, Doctor!

413
00:24:51,094 --> 00:24:52,360
Hello, Shazdeh.

414
00:24:52,400 --> 00:24:53,580
Bless you.

415
00:24:53,620 --> 00:24:55,540
Uncle Asdollah, how is he? Is it bad?

416
00:24:55,580 --> 00:24:57,784
Have you circumcised this one yet?

417
00:24:57,834 --> 00:25:00,320
Not yet. He's still little.

418
00:25:03,014 --> 00:25:04,564
Shut up, boy.

419
00:25:05,234 --> 00:25:08,000
If you'd had the nerve for one measly trip to San Francisco,

420
00:25:08,040 --> 00:25:09,400
none of this would've happened.

421
00:25:09,440 --> 00:25:11,360
Uncle Asdollah, I've decided...

422
00:25:11,400 --> 00:25:13,860
To pay San Francisco a visit? Bravo!

423
00:25:13,900 --> 00:25:17,600
Couldn't you decide two days sooner and spare the old man this?

424
00:25:17,640 --> 00:25:20,644
No, Uncle Asdollah, I've decided to kill myself.

425
00:25:20,694 --> 00:25:24,360
Oh, bravo! Good decision. When, God willing?

426
00:25:24,400 --> 00:25:27,420
I'm serious, Uncle Asdollah. I can't look Leili in the eye.

427
00:25:27,460 --> 00:25:30,600
Moment... moment... Have you picked the method?

428
00:25:30,640 --> 00:25:32,580
Not yet, but I'll find one.

429
00:25:32,620 --> 00:25:35,744
Find a method that doesn't hurt.

430
00:25:35,794 --> 00:25:36,680
God rest your soul.

431
00:25:36,720 --> 00:25:38,530
You were a good boy.

432
00:25:39,974 --> 00:25:41,460
There's nothing to worry about.

433
00:25:41,500 --> 00:25:43,115
Bless you.

434
00:25:44,754 --> 00:25:48,760
His heart's beating a bit irregularly.

435
00:25:48,800 --> 00:25:51,240
You must get him to the hospital.

436
00:25:51,280 --> 00:25:55,170
Doctor, in this state, isn't it dangerous to move him?

437
00:25:55,780 --> 00:26:00,440
Better than just sitting on your hands watching him.

438
00:26:02,620 --> 00:26:04,620
What happened?

439
00:26:06,300 --> 00:26:09,100
Where am I?

440
00:26:09,300 --> 00:26:12,620
Where are you taking me?

441
00:26:12,700 --> 00:26:14,650
Brother, you were taken ill.

442
00:26:14,700 --> 00:26:17,410
The doctor said to take you to the hospital.

443
00:26:17,460 --> 00:26:18,690
Spread this blanket.

444
00:26:18,740 --> 00:26:20,290
Hospital?

445
00:26:20,340 --> 00:26:23,820
Bless you, bless you.

446
00:26:23,900 --> 00:26:25,900
It's nothing serious.

447
00:26:26,620 --> 00:26:30,220
We may need some equipment

448
00:26:30,260 --> 00:26:31,940
that we don't have here.

449
00:26:31,980 --> 00:26:33,790
Yes, lift him up.

450
00:26:34,514 --> 00:26:39,840
Who told you to take me to the hospital?

451
00:26:39,914 --> 00:26:41,004
Easy, easy.

452
00:26:41,054 --> 00:26:47,020
How dare you try to take me to the hospital!

453
00:26:47,060 --> 00:26:51,440
You want to throw the lamb to the wolves!

454
00:26:52,334 --> 00:26:56,820
You want to hand me over to the English!

455
00:26:56,860 --> 00:26:58,460
Gently! Gently!

456
00:26:58,500 --> 00:27:00,180
Lift him.

457
00:27:02,254 --> 00:27:07,894
Don't! Don't take me! Put me down!

458
00:27:09,334 --> 00:27:11,599
Oh, my God, what happened?

459
00:27:14,140 --> 00:27:16,210
Gently, carefully.

460
00:27:17,674 --> 00:27:19,809
Me... hospital...

461
00:27:20,050 --> 00:27:21,424
Dear Uncle! Dear Uncle!

462
00:27:21,474 --> 00:27:23,609
My God, Brother's dead!

463
00:27:25,760 --> 00:27:26,560
What happened?

464
00:27:26,600 --> 00:27:30,260
Bless you, what did you expect? Bring him.

465
00:27:30,600 --> 00:27:32,050
Yes, Doctor.

466
00:27:32,100 --> 00:27:34,060
What's wrong, Sir? God forbid!

467
00:27:34,100 --> 00:27:35,220
Bless you.

468
00:27:35,260 --> 00:27:40,940
Help me, brother, help me!

469
00:27:40,980 --> 00:27:44,060
In this city, where on every side

470
00:27:44,100 --> 00:27:49,060
the English are itching to kill me,

471
00:27:49,100 --> 00:27:52,560
these people want to take me to the hospital.

472
00:27:52,600 --> 00:27:55,060
Don't let them take me.

473
00:27:55,100 --> 00:27:57,060
You'll do no such thing!

474
00:27:57,100 --> 00:27:59,664
As long as the English are in this city,

475
00:27:59,714 --> 00:28:01,784
it's unwise to take Sir to the hospital.

476
00:28:01,834 --> 00:28:05,060
The doctor said Brother has to go to the hospital.

477
00:28:05,100 --> 00:28:07,060
Well, let the doctors come to his house.

478
00:28:07,100 --> 00:28:09,060
They may need equipment they lack at home.

479
00:28:09,100 --> 00:28:11,060
Put him down, put him down.

480
00:28:11,100 --> 00:28:12,060
Yes, sir.

481
00:28:12,100 --> 00:28:14,820
Bring whatever equipment is needed to the house.

482
00:28:16,100 --> 00:28:21,100
Everyone tried to make his fear of English revenge look like a fantasy,

483
00:28:21,794 --> 00:28:25,794
and that was something Dear Uncle could never accept.

484
00:28:26,154 --> 00:28:28,160
In all our big family,

485
00:28:28,200 --> 00:28:31,140
there was no one who understood what he truly believed,

486
00:28:31,180 --> 00:28:32,860
except one person.

487
00:28:33,254 --> 00:28:35,194
And that was Agha Jan.

488
00:28:35,754 --> 00:28:38,060
Dear Uncle Napoleon, to survive,

489
00:28:38,100 --> 00:28:40,820
had come to lean on the moral support of a man

490
00:28:40,860 --> 00:28:43,255
who wanted to destroy him.

491
00:28:44,354 --> 00:28:46,229
It was a bitter fate.

492
00:28:59,014 --> 00:28:59,724
Who is it?

493
00:28:59,774 --> 00:29:00,904
It's me. Open the door.

494
00:29:00,954 --> 00:29:02,124
Shazdeh's not home.

495
00:29:02,174 --> 00:29:03,464
I have urgent business with him.

496
00:29:03,504 --> 00:29:04,114
Open the door.

497
00:29:04,154 --> 00:29:05,524
Why do you want to come in?

498
00:29:05,574 --> 00:29:07,224
He went out early this morning.

499
00:29:07,274 --> 00:29:08,824
I know he's home.

500
00:29:08,874 --> 00:29:09,624
He's not.

501
00:29:09,674 --> 00:29:11,240
Nanny! Nanny!

502
00:29:11,294 --> 00:29:12,094
Yes?

503
00:29:12,134 --> 00:29:13,294
Nanny!

504
00:29:14,314 --> 00:29:15,114
Yes?

505
00:29:15,154 --> 00:29:16,204
Let him in.

506
00:29:16,254 --> 00:29:16,984
Yes, sir.

507
00:29:17,024 --> 00:29:17,994
Put him in that room.

508
00:29:18,034 --> 00:29:18,834
Yes, sir.

509
00:29:20,154 --> 00:29:21,204
It's important.

510
00:29:21,254 --> 00:29:22,284
Where is he?

511
00:29:22,334 --> 00:29:24,534
Right here. In this room.

512
00:29:25,194 --> 00:29:27,719
Uncle Asdollah! Uncle Asdollah!

513
00:29:28,734 --> 00:29:29,764
Go in that room till I come.

514
00:29:29,814 --> 00:29:31,544
Uncle Asdollah, please come out!

515
00:29:31,594 --> 00:29:33,264
Moment, I'm getting dressed.

516
00:29:33,314 --> 00:29:35,124
Never mind, open the door, I'll come in.

517
00:29:35,174 --> 00:29:37,284
Someone's life is in danger, Uncle Asdollah!

518
00:29:37,334 --> 00:29:40,684
Hey, stop shouting. I said go in that room till I come.

519
00:29:40,734 --> 00:29:43,050
I'll be there before you've packed your suicide kit.

520
00:29:43,100 --> 00:29:45,625
You're joking again.

521
00:30:36,004 --> 00:30:38,160
Stop staring at that photo.

522
00:30:38,200 --> 00:30:39,020
Who's in the photo?

523
00:30:39,060 --> 00:30:40,854
An old friend.

524
00:30:40,904 --> 00:30:43,540
So, you've decided to kill yourself today, God bless you?

525
00:30:43,580 --> 00:30:46,894
I thought by now you'd gone to San Francisco and changed your mind.

526
00:30:46,944 --> 00:30:48,020
Uncle Asdollah, this is really...

527
00:30:48,060 --> 00:30:50,900
Actually, you're right. A man who won't go to San Francisco

528
00:30:50,940 --> 00:30:52,700
has no business left in this world.

529
00:30:52,740 --> 00:30:54,580
The sooner you kill yourself, the better.

530
00:30:54,620 --> 00:30:58,254
Uncle Asdollah, Leili's so upset I'd completely forgotten.

531
00:30:58,304 --> 00:31:00,740
She expects things of you, the poor girl.

532
00:31:00,780 --> 00:31:03,340
A little San Francisco, a little Los Angeles.

533
00:31:03,380 --> 00:31:04,340
Uncle Asdollah!

534
00:31:04,380 --> 00:31:06,340
The poor thing's rotting away in this dump.

535
00:31:06,380 --> 00:31:08,340
Uncle Asdollah, I didn't come about Leili.

536
00:31:08,380 --> 00:31:10,340
The trouble is poor Mash Ghasem.

537
00:31:10,380 --> 00:31:12,340
Oh, moment, moment!

538
00:31:12,380 --> 00:31:14,340
Mash Ghasem went to San Francisco?

539
00:31:14,380 --> 00:31:16,340
Has that Ghias Abadi been learning from you too?

540
00:31:16,380 --> 00:31:18,340
Dear Uncle is furious, absolutely raging.

541
00:31:18,380 --> 00:31:20,340
He wants to kill Mash Ghasem.

542
00:31:20,380 --> 00:31:21,340
Over San Francisco?

543
00:31:21,380 --> 00:31:24,815
No, because he says he's an English spy.

544
00:31:29,380 --> 00:31:31,380
Cheers.

545
00:31:33,380 --> 00:31:38,240
He must have found Churchill's telegram to Ghias Abad in his pocket.

546
00:31:38,280 --> 00:31:40,080
I don't know how it happened either.

547
00:31:40,120 --> 00:31:42,320
Leili came and told me a few minutes ago.

548
00:31:42,360 --> 00:31:44,720
This morning Dear Uncle suddenly snapped.

549
00:31:44,760 --> 00:31:46,300
He went after Mash Ghasem with a gun.

550
00:31:46,340 --> 00:31:48,740
He ran off in terror and hid in the kitchen,

551
00:31:48,780 --> 00:31:50,360
and locked the door behind him.

552
00:31:50,400 --> 00:31:53,080
And you came straight to me first thing.

553
00:31:53,120 --> 00:31:54,740
Agha Jan went out early this morning.

554
00:31:54,780 --> 00:31:56,620
Well, I'm busy this morning too.

555
00:31:56,660 --> 00:31:57,820
Uncle Asdollah, please!

556
00:31:57,860 --> 00:32:01,860
Dear Uncle's sitting there with the rifle, waiting for Mash Ghasem to come out.

557
00:32:01,964 --> 00:32:02,800
Strange.

558
00:32:02,840 --> 00:32:05,500
All right, you go, I'll come in an hour.

559
00:32:05,540 --> 00:32:07,654
I'm afraid it'll be too late, Uncle Asdollah.

560
00:32:07,704 --> 00:32:09,900
But I can't, I'm busy.

561
00:32:09,940 --> 00:32:10,940
I have an appointment,

562
00:32:10,980 --> 00:32:13,260
the master builder is supposed to come here.

563
00:32:13,300 --> 00:32:15,640
The kitchen ceiling is caving in.

564
00:32:15,684 --> 00:32:17,714
Uncle, leave word for him to wait till you're back.

565
00:32:17,764 --> 00:32:19,114
Mash Ghasem's life is at stake.

566
00:32:19,164 --> 00:32:22,600
Unbelievable! I tell him it's a bull, he says milk it.

567
00:32:22,640 --> 00:32:24,640
Asdollah, where are you?

568
00:32:25,764 --> 00:32:28,584
Uncle Asdollah, who's this?

569
00:32:28,624 --> 00:32:30,074
Lady, what is it?

570
00:32:30,124 --> 00:32:32,430
Uncle Asdollah, looks like the builder's here!

571
00:32:32,480 --> 00:32:35,340
Don't talk nonsense. This builder isn't Iranian, she's foreign.

572
00:32:35,380 --> 00:32:36,840
Wait in the yard till I'm dressed.

573
00:32:36,880 --> 00:32:39,535
Go, don't stand there... go, I'm coming, go.

574
00:32:39,804 --> 00:32:41,700
Go on, sir, go.

575
00:32:42,064 --> 00:32:43,940
What's this, Lady? You've disgraced me!

576
00:32:43,980 --> 00:32:46,505
Lucky Saeid is one of us.

577
00:32:59,244 --> 00:33:02,100
Since the day Akhtar was to test Pouri

578
00:33:02,140 --> 00:33:03,900
and I set off that firecracker,

579
00:33:03,940 --> 00:33:05,880
several weeks had passed.

580
00:33:06,944 --> 00:33:09,440
Dear Uncle Napoleon was in a bad way.

581
00:33:09,480 --> 00:33:14,840
A heart doctor and a nerve doctor were treating him at home.

582
00:33:16,604 --> 00:33:20,760
The heart doctor sincerely believed his illness was of the heart,

583
00:33:20,800 --> 00:33:26,560
and the nerve doctor honestly believed the heart doctor knew nothing.

584
00:33:27,864 --> 00:33:30,240
I'd forgotten my own troubles,

585
00:33:30,280 --> 00:33:33,455
I was so worried about Leili in those days.

586
00:33:33,724 --> 00:33:35,934
What's up? What were you thinking about?

587
00:33:35,984 --> 00:33:37,040
Nothing, just thinking.

588
00:33:37,080 --> 00:33:39,040
About Dr. Naser-ol-Hokama.

589
00:33:39,080 --> 00:33:41,040
By the way, how's that Arabian stallion, Pouri the Dud?

590
00:33:41,080 --> 00:33:43,040
Your firecracker sorted him out, eh?

591
00:33:43,080 --> 00:33:47,040
I don't know. I think he's seeing Dr. Naser-ol-Hokama regularly again.

592
00:33:47,080 --> 00:33:51,080
As long as Dr. Naser-ol-Hokama treats Pouri, rest assured, Leili's safe.

593
00:33:51,784 --> 00:33:55,400
Naser-ol-Hokama has treated himself for forty years and still has no child.

594
00:33:55,440 --> 00:33:57,560
Both his wives divorced him.

595
00:33:57,600 --> 00:34:00,880
This third one would have too, if not for that ass Dustali.

596
00:34:00,920 --> 00:34:02,100
Uncle Asdollah!

597
00:34:02,140 --> 00:34:04,380
The doctor does have a child by this wife.

598
00:34:04,420 --> 00:34:06,580
Moment, moment!

599
00:34:06,620 --> 00:34:09,015
Did he give birth to it himself?

600
00:34:40,984 --> 00:34:42,084
Uncle Asdollah.

601
00:34:42,124 --> 00:34:44,534
Hey, don't be upset, dear. Don't cry.

602
00:34:44,584 --> 00:34:46,714
It's all right, Leili dear.

603
00:34:46,764 --> 00:34:48,274
Anything new?

604
00:34:48,324 --> 00:34:50,324
No, Mash Ghasem is still in the basement.

605
00:34:50,364 --> 00:34:51,204
And Dear Uncle?

606
00:34:51,244 --> 00:34:54,030
Still sitting in the yard with the rifle, by the basement door,

607
00:34:54,080 --> 00:34:56,840
muttering things I can't understand.

608
00:34:56,880 --> 00:34:58,105
Moment!

609
00:34:59,080 --> 00:35:01,040
All right, let me go first, and you two

610
00:35:01,080 --> 00:35:04,320
follow behind, so it's not obvious why we came.

611
00:35:05,080 --> 00:35:06,040
Saeid.

612
00:35:06,080 --> 00:35:08,040
Enough, nothing's happened yet.

613
00:35:08,080 --> 00:35:11,040
Well, well! Going hunting, God willing?

614
00:35:11,080 --> 00:35:13,040
Which way are you headed?

615
00:35:13,080 --> 00:35:15,634
Oh, I quite forgot hunting season is over.

616
00:35:15,684 --> 00:35:18,040
Not at all! It's hunting season, all right.

617
00:35:18,080 --> 00:35:20,920
The season for hunting spies and lackeys of the English.

618
00:35:21,120 --> 00:35:23,030
Do you mean me?

619
00:35:23,080 --> 00:35:25,240
No, I didn't mean you.

620
00:35:25,780 --> 00:35:28,040
But perhaps that's so as well.

621
00:35:28,080 --> 00:35:30,240
Who knows? Maybe you, too,

622
00:35:30,280 --> 00:35:33,580
will one day turn out to be their lackey.

623
00:35:35,920 --> 00:35:40,280
Who'd have thought the English could buy Ghasem?

624
00:35:41,080 --> 00:35:45,420
Who'd have thought Ghasem would stab me in the back?

625
00:35:45,460 --> 00:35:47,740
Moment, moment!

626
00:35:48,080 --> 00:35:51,580
Mash Ghasem betrayed you?

627
00:35:51,620 --> 00:35:52,600
Is that so, Sir?

628
00:35:52,640 --> 00:35:54,760
And what a betrayal!

629
00:35:54,800 --> 00:35:59,800
It makes Marshal Grouchy's betrayal of Napoleon look like a favor.

630
00:35:59,840 --> 00:36:05,620
Grouchy could have gone to Waterloo to help his master, but didn't.

631
00:36:05,660 --> 00:36:08,720
But at least he didn't stab him in the back.

632
00:36:08,760 --> 00:36:10,380
Very strange.

633
00:36:11,104 --> 00:36:14,344
I'd have suspected anyone but Mash Ghasem.

634
00:36:14,924 --> 00:36:17,060
After all the kindness you showed him!

635
00:36:17,100 --> 00:36:19,950
Lord God, you save us!

636
00:36:20,804 --> 00:36:22,159
A man for whose sake

637
00:36:22,824 --> 00:36:27,140
I risked my own life time and again!

638
00:36:28,180 --> 00:36:31,740
I saved his filthy life in battle!

639
00:36:31,780 --> 00:36:34,560
And he betrays me like this!

640
00:36:34,600 --> 00:36:37,600
Sells himself to the English!

641
00:36:37,640 --> 00:36:39,560
How did you uncover his betrayal?

642
00:36:39,600 --> 00:36:40,960
I grew suspicious of him.

643
00:36:41,000 --> 00:36:43,600
This morning I caught him red-handed.

644
00:36:43,640 --> 00:36:44,865
I see.

645
00:36:44,940 --> 00:36:46,425
He confessed.

646
00:36:46,760 --> 00:36:52,860
Do you hear? With his own cursed tongue he confessed he's an English lackey.

647
00:36:52,900 --> 00:36:56,840
Sir put the rifle right to our gut and said, confess,

648
00:36:56,880 --> 00:36:59,275
if you don't, we'll kill you.

649
00:37:00,540 --> 00:37:02,220
So we confessed.

650
00:37:02,260 --> 00:37:04,265
Shut up, you treacherous hireling!

651
00:37:04,840 --> 00:37:05,760
Saeid, run!

652
00:37:05,800 --> 00:37:07,540
Foreigner-lover!

653
00:37:07,580 --> 00:37:09,220
Please, Sir, don't shoot, the children are scared.

654
00:37:09,260 --> 00:37:12,300
I must give this traitor what he deserves.

655
00:37:12,340 --> 00:37:16,230
Please stay calm. Be forgiving.

656
00:37:16,800 --> 00:37:19,210
Dear Uncle, have mercy on him. Poor thing.

657
00:37:19,260 --> 00:37:20,690
Daddy, I swear...

658
00:37:20,740 --> 00:37:23,560
You're right. I know, I know. I understand.

659
00:37:23,600 --> 00:37:24,400
Daddy.

660
00:37:24,440 --> 00:37:25,720
I do understand, believe me.

661
00:37:25,760 --> 00:37:28,675
Kid, bring a glass of water. Run.

662
00:37:30,000 --> 00:37:32,150
He must be punished.

663
00:37:32,200 --> 00:37:35,580
He must get what he deserves.

664
00:37:35,640 --> 00:37:37,010
He must face the...

665
00:37:37,060 --> 00:37:38,330
Mash Ghasem.

666
00:37:38,380 --> 00:37:39,995
Come on out.

667
00:37:41,300 --> 00:37:42,655
Mash Ghasem!

668
00:37:42,840 --> 00:37:44,325
Come out.

669
00:37:44,840 --> 00:37:46,120
Kiss Sir's hand.

670
00:37:46,160 --> 00:37:47,160
Repent.

671
00:37:47,200 --> 00:37:48,540
We've nothing to say, Sir.

672
00:37:48,580 --> 00:37:50,720
You tell Sir to spare us.

673
00:37:50,760 --> 00:37:52,760
We're Sir's servant.

674
00:37:52,800 --> 00:37:54,675
We're Sir's humble servant.

675
00:38:01,680 --> 00:38:02,840
Here.

676
00:38:03,400 --> 00:38:05,405
Drink, Daddy, drink.

677
00:38:05,900 --> 00:38:07,740
Mash Ghasem, this is no use.

678
00:38:07,780 --> 00:38:09,300
Sir has found out everything.

679
00:38:09,340 --> 00:38:12,140
Come out, shame on you.

680
00:38:12,880 --> 00:38:14,540
You'd better repent your sin.

681
00:38:14,580 --> 00:38:16,020
Come on out.

682
00:38:16,060 --> 00:38:18,000
Honestly, Sir, why should I lie?

683
00:38:18,040 --> 00:38:20,460
I swear to God, we didn't do a thing.

684
00:38:20,500 --> 00:38:24,400
We did no wrong, Sir's angry at us for nothing.

685
00:38:26,160 --> 00:38:28,880
If you don't starve to death in that toilet,

686
00:38:28,920 --> 00:38:32,140
I'll fill that empty skull of yours with lead.

687
00:38:32,180 --> 00:38:32,800
More, Dear Uncle.

688
00:38:32,840 --> 00:38:35,365
A little more, Daddy.

689
00:38:35,740 --> 00:38:40,910
Sir, I swear by Saint Abbas, if we ever laid eyes on one of them Englishes...

690
00:38:40,960 --> 00:38:42,840
Mash Ghasem, don't be insolent!

691
00:38:42,880 --> 00:38:44,320
Say you're sorry.

692
00:38:44,360 --> 00:38:47,180
We fought the English alongside Sir.

693
00:38:47,220 --> 00:38:50,655
Now we're to say we're an English spy?

694
00:38:51,680 --> 00:38:52,980
God forgive us! God forbid!

695
00:38:53,020 --> 00:38:55,805
Sir, why do you slander us so?

696
00:38:56,280 --> 00:39:00,280
Then how could we look the Ghias Abadis in the eye?

697
00:39:01,000 --> 00:39:04,695
Every Ghias Abadi thirsts for English blood.

698
00:39:06,580 --> 00:39:08,120
We ourselves, back in Ghias Abad,

699
00:39:08,160 --> 00:39:10,100
had a fellow townsman...

700
00:39:11,700 --> 00:39:13,150
Drink, Dear Uncle.

701
00:39:13,200 --> 00:39:14,540
That's enough.

702
00:39:18,440 --> 00:39:19,890
Drink. A little more.

703
00:39:19,940 --> 00:39:21,630
Treason...

704
00:39:21,680 --> 00:39:23,555
Drink, Dear Uncle.

705
00:39:23,700 --> 00:39:25,520
I'll kill you!

706
00:39:30,800 --> 00:39:32,400
A little more.

707
00:39:32,440 --> 00:39:34,705
Daddy, please.

708
00:39:36,760 --> 00:39:39,860
Mash Ghasem's lost his mind completely, just like Sir.

709
00:39:39,900 --> 00:39:41,800
I'll skin you alive!

710
00:39:46,180 --> 00:39:50,180
If Mash Ghasem holds out another hour or two, maybe there's a way.

711
00:39:50,880 --> 00:39:52,560
What way?

712
00:39:53,200 --> 00:39:56,830
The only way to the toilet is through that locked door.

713
00:39:59,580 --> 00:40:02,050
What a funny idea! My boy, if

714
00:40:02,100 --> 00:40:06,490
he needs the toilet, he'll go to your house, do his business and come back.

715
00:40:06,540 --> 00:40:10,540
Then we'll get Mash Ghasem out. What's funny about that?

716
00:40:11,780 --> 00:40:15,780
Pretending Dear Uncle was unwell, I've got five glasses of water into him.

717
00:40:18,440 --> 00:40:20,705
Then let's sit and wait for results.

718
00:40:24,440 --> 00:40:26,440
I'll skin you alive!

719
00:40:38,400 --> 00:40:41,180
Ugh... I'll skin you alive, I swear.

720
00:40:44,900 --> 00:40:45,950
Leili, tell

721
00:40:46,000 --> 00:40:49,100
Naneh Belgheys to fill the water jug and put it in the toilet.

722
00:40:49,150 --> 00:40:52,650
Daddy, Mash Ghasem's locked that place too.

723
00:40:53,180 --> 00:40:55,350
Dear Uncle, shall I take you to our house?

724
00:40:55,400 --> 00:40:56,495
No.

725
00:41:03,100 --> 00:41:05,235
If I don't skin you alive...

726
00:41:11,600 --> 00:41:14,400
Naneh Belgheys! Naneh Belgheys!

727
00:41:15,340 --> 00:41:16,900
Yes, sir.

728
00:41:18,860 --> 00:41:20,820
Did you want something?

729
00:41:21,360 --> 00:41:24,090
Take that flowerpot of mine and put it in the room.

730
00:41:24,140 --> 00:41:26,280
Yes, sir.

731
00:41:55,800 --> 00:41:57,090
There you go.

732
00:41:57,340 --> 00:42:00,385
The jailer relieved himself in a flowerpot.

733
00:42:01,020 --> 00:42:03,670
Any other bright ideas?

734
00:42:03,720 --> 00:42:04,815
No.

735
00:42:05,440 --> 00:42:06,050
Don't sulk.

736
00:42:06,100 --> 00:42:08,990
Maybe I'll think of a way.

737
00:42:09,040 --> 00:42:11,110
If only your Agha Jan were here.

738
00:42:11,160 --> 00:42:12,370
What could Agha Jan do?

739
00:42:12,420 --> 00:42:13,970
Plenty.

740
00:42:14,360 --> 00:42:18,420
He's the only one who never burst your Dear Uncle's bubble about the English.

741
00:42:19,180 --> 00:42:21,490
Right now Sir needs the whole world to believe

742
00:42:21,540 --> 00:42:26,400
the British Empire thinks of nothing but destroying him.

743
00:42:26,440 --> 00:42:30,400
At times like this, nobody can buck him up like your Agha Jan.

744
00:42:30,440 --> 00:42:33,400
Well, since Agha Jan isn't here, why don't you try?

745
00:42:33,440 --> 00:42:36,400
I'm afraid I can't handle this lunatic alone.

746
00:42:36,440 --> 00:42:38,400
You'll have to help me.

747
00:42:38,440 --> 00:42:40,400
Have you read Napoleon's life story?

748
00:42:40,440 --> 00:42:43,400
Yes, Uncle Asdollah, Dear Uncle gave me the book himself.

749
00:42:43,440 --> 00:42:45,440
Then open your ears and listen to what I say.

750
00:42:45,490 --> 00:42:47,900
I want you to tell your Dear Uncle this... this story.

751
00:42:47,950 --> 00:42:49,695
Got it?

752
00:43:20,490 --> 00:43:23,600
Uncle Asdollah, there's always been treachery in the world.

753
00:43:23,650 --> 00:43:26,890
Didn't Marshal Ney betray Napoleon?

754
00:43:27,090 --> 00:43:30,510
Marshal Ney betrayed Napoleon, but later,

755
00:43:30,550 --> 00:43:36,240
when the chance came, he made up for his sin with his service.

756
00:43:36,290 --> 00:43:37,360
Really!

757
00:43:37,410 --> 00:43:40,250
You read the book, how can you not remember?

758
00:43:40,300 --> 00:43:41,850
I don't remember.

759
00:43:42,070 --> 00:43:45,760
When he came back from Elba, they sent Marshal Ney to fight him.

760
00:43:45,810 --> 00:43:50,870
The moment he saw Napoleon, he dismounted and kissed his hand,

761
00:43:51,430 --> 00:43:55,880
and handed his sword in surrender to his master.

762
00:43:55,930 --> 00:43:57,030
Quiet!

763
00:43:58,650 --> 00:44:03,540
Ah, now I remember. Later he proved his loyalty to Napoleon with his death.

764
00:44:03,590 --> 00:44:04,400
In fact, traitors like that...

765
00:44:04,440 --> 00:44:07,900
Traitors like that, once they regret their treachery,

766
00:44:07,940 --> 00:44:11,560
become more loyal and devoted than many servants.

767
00:44:12,550 --> 00:44:17,840
Of course, it was Napoleon's mercy that made Ney so devoted.

768
00:44:17,880 --> 00:44:24,110
Yes, I've tested this many times myself in my wars.

769
00:44:24,160 --> 00:44:26,080
It was always so.

770
00:44:26,120 --> 00:44:30,560
When I granted quarter to the enemy commander,

771
00:44:31,290 --> 00:44:38,300
all at once that enmity turned into sincere friendship.

772
00:44:38,340 --> 00:44:42,940
Now in my view, this Mash Ghasem is only human, after all.

773
00:44:42,980 --> 00:44:45,050
He was weak, they tricked him.

774
00:44:45,910 --> 00:44:50,080
It takes great self-confidence not to fall into the English trap.

775
00:44:51,190 --> 00:44:55,015
You yourself, Sir, if you didn't have such strength and stamina,

776
00:44:55,270 --> 00:44:57,940
do you think the English wouldn't have tricked you?

777
00:44:57,980 --> 00:44:59,000
Impossible.

778
00:44:59,040 --> 00:45:00,340
Why?

779
00:45:01,490 --> 00:45:03,580
How many times they tried!

780
00:45:03,620 --> 00:45:05,660
What promises!

781
00:45:05,700 --> 00:45:07,720
What tricks!

782
00:45:07,760 --> 00:45:10,440
What money they promised!

783
00:45:10,480 --> 00:45:14,160
What beautiful women they sent!

784
00:45:14,200 --> 00:45:17,240
What didn't they try!

785
00:45:17,950 --> 00:45:19,540
Anyone else in your place,

786
00:45:19,580 --> 00:45:21,420
do you think he wouldn't have given in?

787
00:45:21,460 --> 00:45:22,600
Wouldn't be fooled?

788
00:45:22,640 --> 00:45:26,080
Of course! He'd have been fooled.

789
00:45:26,120 --> 00:45:29,560
I have no doubt he'd have been corrupted.

790
00:45:29,600 --> 00:45:33,400
Well, then, what do you expect of a poor peasant servant

791
00:45:33,440 --> 00:45:36,400
who can barely make ends meet?

792
00:45:36,440 --> 00:45:39,440
They tricked the poor man, they fooled him.

793
00:45:40,640 --> 00:45:44,560
But this good-for-nothing refuses

794
00:45:44,600 --> 00:45:47,040
to confess honestly,

795
00:45:47,080 --> 00:45:48,960
to ask forgiveness,

796
00:45:49,000 --> 00:45:52,400
to say he's sorry.

797
00:45:56,630 --> 00:46:01,660
Sir, the poor devil is locked in the toilet.

798
00:46:02,490 --> 00:46:05,900
And you're lying in wait for him with a rifle, like a hunter.

799
00:46:05,940 --> 00:46:08,860
What do you expect of him?

800
00:46:08,900 --> 00:46:12,500
Let him come out and I'll have a word with him.

801
00:46:12,540 --> 00:46:16,040
Then you'll see for yourself he's sorry as a whipped dog.

802
00:46:18,550 --> 00:46:20,380
Very well, we'll see.

803
00:46:28,690 --> 00:46:32,100
Come, Leili dear, help your father go inside,

804
00:46:32,150 --> 00:46:36,150
while I bring that poor dupe to throw himself at his feet.

805
00:46:36,650 --> 00:46:38,380
Please, please, go ahead.

806
00:46:38,430 --> 00:46:39,920
Take this.

807
00:46:39,970 --> 00:46:41,390
Please.

808
00:46:43,090 --> 00:46:44,575
You go too.

809
00:46:47,290 --> 00:46:49,700
Leili dear, you go to your room too.

810
00:46:49,740 --> 00:46:51,420
Yes, Daddy.

811
00:46:53,390 --> 00:46:55,525
Saeid dear, look after her.

812
00:47:03,930 --> 00:47:05,860
Mash Ghasem, listen to what I tell you.

813
00:47:05,900 --> 00:47:08,860
Open up. Sir has forgiven your sin.

814
00:47:08,900 --> 00:47:10,840
But Sir, truth is, we're scared.

815
00:47:10,880 --> 00:47:11,680
Well?

816
00:47:11,720 --> 00:47:15,120
I said open the door, Mash Ghasem, come on, be brave.

817
00:47:15,160 --> 00:47:16,255
Come.

818
00:47:16,910 --> 00:47:19,120
Sir promised to leave you be.

819
00:47:19,160 --> 00:47:19,960
Mash Ghasem.

820
00:47:20,000 --> 00:47:22,020
Come, dear, I've got your back. Come.

821
00:47:22,060 --> 00:47:23,480
Come on, man.

822
00:47:26,930 --> 00:47:30,600
Saint Moslem, help me! Help me!

823
00:47:32,910 --> 00:47:37,060
Sir, will you allow Mash Ghasem to kiss your hand?

824
00:47:38,260 --> 00:47:41,435
He has to answer a few questions of mine.

825
00:47:42,510 --> 00:47:45,230
He'll answer any question you ask.

826
00:47:45,650 --> 00:47:51,480
First he must say where the English made contact with him.

827
00:47:52,710 --> 00:47:57,640
Hear that, Mash Ghasem? Where did the English contact you?

828
00:47:58,370 --> 00:48:02,260
Honestly, Sir, why lie? In-in-in-inside the bakery.

829
00:48:03,070 --> 00:48:05,800
Have him say when.

830
00:48:07,270 --> 00:48:08,100
When?

831
00:48:08,140 --> 00:48:11,820
Well, it was Tuesday, no, Wednesday last week.

832
00:48:12,630 --> 00:48:13,360
It was Wednesday.

833
00:48:13,400 --> 00:48:15,400
How did they contact him?

834
00:48:15,980 --> 00:48:17,860
How did they contact you?

835
00:48:17,900 --> 00:48:19,120
Honestly, Sir, why lie?

836
00:48:19,160 --> 00:48:20,640
The grave is this close!

837
00:48:20,680 --> 00:48:22,460
We'd gone to buy bread.

838
00:48:22,500 --> 00:48:24,720
All of a sudden we saw one of them Englishes

839
00:48:24,760 --> 00:48:27,480
eyeing us funny from the alley.

840
00:48:27,520 --> 00:48:30,110
Now and then he'd give us a wink.

841
00:48:30,160 --> 00:48:31,680
Honestly, at first we thought

842
00:48:31,720 --> 00:48:34,300
that Englishman, no offense, had designs on us.

843
00:48:34,340 --> 00:48:36,100
As our fellow townsman used to say...

844
00:48:36,140 --> 00:48:38,430
Tell him not to digress.

845
00:48:39,102 --> 00:48:40,720
Don't digress, Mash Ghasem.

846
00:48:40,760 --> 00:48:42,500
Tell how they made contact.

847
00:48:42,540 --> 00:48:44,580
Well, Sir, they made contact, that's all.

848
00:48:44,620 --> 00:48:46,900
Somehow we didn't even notice ourselves.

849
00:48:46,940 --> 00:48:50,220
Before we could pull ourselves together, they'd got us.

850
00:48:50,260 --> 00:48:52,500
Got us, that's all.

851
00:48:52,540 --> 00:48:55,240
Have him say, for killing me,

852
00:48:55,280 --> 00:48:58,960
how much money did they promise him?

853
00:48:59,000 --> 00:49:01,740
God forgive us! Us kill Sir?

854
00:49:01,780 --> 00:49:03,740
May our hands wither!

855
00:49:03,780 --> 00:49:07,240
No, Sir, there was never any question of assassination.

856
00:49:07,280 --> 00:49:09,780
He only carried news of you to them.

857
00:49:09,820 --> 00:49:10,820
Why?

858
00:49:10,860 --> 00:49:13,060
Now what does he intend to do?

859
00:49:13,520 --> 00:49:15,980
Well, well, well, Sir, we, we, we wanted to...

860
00:49:16,020 --> 00:49:18,660
Sir means, are you sorry?

861
00:49:18,700 --> 00:49:21,940
Or do you still want to serve the English?

862
00:49:22,220 --> 00:49:24,580
God forbid we'd serve them!

863
00:49:24,620 --> 00:49:28,410
We'll curse their mothers and send them packing to their fathers.

864
00:49:28,460 --> 00:49:30,660
We'll tell them we won't eat their bread.

865
00:49:30,700 --> 00:49:32,260
We serve our own master.

866
00:49:32,300 --> 00:49:35,420
When does he intend to give them that answer?

867
00:49:35,460 --> 00:49:38,960
I swear by Saint Abbas, we...

868
00:49:39,000 --> 00:49:41,380
Answer, Mash Ghasem.

869
00:49:41,420 --> 00:49:43,980
When will you send them packing? Today, or...

870
00:49:44,020 --> 00:49:44,660
Say today.

871
00:49:44,700 --> 00:49:45,840
This very day.

872
00:49:45,880 --> 00:49:47,755
No, no, right now.

873
00:49:48,822 --> 00:49:50,080
Now go kiss Sir's hand.

874
00:49:50,120 --> 00:49:50,460
Yes, sir.

875
00:49:50,500 --> 00:49:51,260
Go.

876
00:49:51,300 --> 00:49:54,420
What can I do? My heart goes out to you.

877
00:49:59,322 --> 00:50:00,417
Sir,

878
00:50:01,042 --> 00:50:02,917
forgive your Ghasem.

879
00:50:10,082 --> 00:50:14,140
For your past services,

880
00:50:14,962 --> 00:50:16,800
I forgive you.

881
00:50:18,162 --> 00:50:21,720
But the first condition is

882
00:50:23,082 --> 00:50:24,502
that you truly

883
00:50:25,602 --> 00:50:28,260
repent of this dirty business

884
00:50:29,202 --> 00:50:31,300
from the bottom of your heart,

885
00:50:33,602 --> 00:50:38,820
and put your strong arms at your master's service.

886
00:50:45,402 --> 00:50:47,797
I'm very worried about Sir.

887
00:50:48,382 --> 00:50:51,980
Hating the English has become a psychological need for him.

888
00:50:52,020 --> 00:50:54,060
Honestly, I'm baffled too.

889
00:50:54,100 --> 00:50:56,980
How does a sane, respectable man end up like this?

890
00:50:57,020 --> 00:50:58,060
Moment!

891
00:50:58,100 --> 00:50:59,612
Moment!

892
00:50:59,662 --> 00:51:03,662
It's very strange that you don't know how he ended up like this!

893
00:51:03,962 --> 00:51:05,680
Anyway, it's happened.

894
00:51:05,720 --> 00:51:07,380
We must act on it soon.

895
00:51:07,420 --> 00:51:08,580
Hello.

896
00:51:08,822 --> 00:51:10,307
Hello, dear.

897
00:51:11,360 --> 00:51:12,270
Close the door.

898
00:51:12,310 --> 00:51:13,470
Yes, sir.

899
00:51:13,690 --> 00:51:14,740
And you go out too.

900
00:51:14,780 --> 00:51:16,460
No, no, let him stay.

901
00:51:16,500 --> 00:51:17,980
Your son isn't a stranger.

902
00:51:18,020 --> 00:51:20,140
He won't tell anyone.

903
00:51:20,180 --> 00:51:20,820
Go sit down.

904
00:51:20,860 --> 00:51:21,780
You know, Shazdeh,

905
00:51:21,820 --> 00:51:24,260
I've told the Colonel several times

906
00:51:24,300 --> 00:51:28,460
that psychiatrists should hold a consultation.

907
00:51:28,860 --> 00:51:29,660
Otherwise...

908
00:51:29,700 --> 00:51:31,900
Moment... don't even think about it.

909
00:51:32,420 --> 00:51:35,380
Even if Sir walked stark naked into Toopkhaneh Square,

910
00:51:35,420 --> 00:51:39,700
the Colonel and the family elders would never call a psychiatrist.

911
00:51:39,740 --> 00:51:45,200
How could Sir, son of the late Sir, grandson of Great Sir, go mad?

912
00:51:45,240 --> 00:51:46,400
God forbid!

913
00:51:46,440 --> 00:51:48,380
So we just sit on our hands

914
00:51:48,420 --> 00:51:51,520
while Sir kills someone for spying for the English

915
00:51:51,560 --> 00:51:53,312
and they haul him off to prison?

916
00:51:53,362 --> 00:51:56,460
Just today, if you'd moved one minute later,

917
00:51:56,500 --> 00:51:59,000
Mash Ghasem's body would be in the morgue now,

918
00:51:59,040 --> 00:52:01,045
and Sir himself in prison.

919
00:52:01,262 --> 00:52:05,460
The law doesn't care about the late Great Sir or the grandson of Little Sir.

920
00:52:05,500 --> 00:52:07,380
They throw the killer in prison,

921
00:52:07,420 --> 00:52:09,200
try him,

922
00:52:09,240 --> 00:52:10,720
hang him.

923
00:52:10,760 --> 00:52:13,100
Put the psychiatrist idea out of your head, sir.

924
00:52:13,140 --> 00:52:17,520
I think the only solution is for the English to lock Sir up for a while.

925
00:52:17,560 --> 00:52:19,560
But you can't force it.

926
00:52:19,600 --> 00:52:21,820
Can we make the English

927
00:52:21,860 --> 00:52:25,500
arrest a retired lieutenant of the Cossack Brigade, eh?

928
00:52:25,540 --> 00:52:28,740
How about Churchill himself coming to apologize to Sir?

929
00:52:28,780 --> 00:52:30,520
Not Churchill,

930
00:52:30,560 --> 00:52:33,060
but if a representative of the English came, maybe.

931
00:52:33,100 --> 00:52:35,820
How about the commander of the British army in Iran?

932
00:52:35,860 --> 00:52:37,880
Or, say, the First Lord of the Admiralty?

933
00:52:37,920 --> 00:52:39,820
Allow me.

934
00:52:39,860 --> 00:52:41,480
We must stage a scene

935
00:52:41,520 --> 00:52:43,520
where someone comes on behalf of the English

936
00:52:43,560 --> 00:52:45,630
to negotiate with Sir.

937
00:52:45,862 --> 00:52:47,640
You're joking, Your Highness.

938
00:52:47,680 --> 00:52:50,420
True, Sir's mind is confused and muddled,

939
00:52:50,460 --> 00:52:53,765
but he's not a child, to believe something like that.

940
00:52:55,162 --> 00:52:56,700
A man who writes fawning letters

941
00:52:56,740 --> 00:52:57,900
to Hitler

942
00:52:57,940 --> 00:53:00,180
and sets up code words with his spies

943
00:53:00,220 --> 00:53:01,640
isn't a child?

944
00:53:02,962 --> 00:53:07,180
When the late Great Sir eats bozbash stew with Marlene Dietrich,

945
00:53:07,220 --> 00:53:09,880
with pickles and golpar to boot,

946
00:53:10,982 --> 00:53:14,937
can't Churchill's envoy come and negotiate with Sir?

947
00:53:15,862 --> 00:53:17,180
Who told you about that?

948
00:53:17,220 --> 00:53:20,560
I was in on it. Sir himself told me once.

949
00:53:20,600 --> 00:53:23,400
Well, we played a little joke once,

950
00:53:23,440 --> 00:53:26,440
and Sir himself... moment, fell for it nicely.

951
00:53:26,480 --> 00:53:28,660
Let's drop all that.

952
00:53:28,700 --> 00:53:33,000
What matters is, are you willing now to help this old man, so that...

953
00:53:33,100 --> 00:53:34,150
So that?

954
00:53:34,200 --> 00:53:38,200
the last few days of his life are less of a torment?

955
00:53:38,300 --> 00:53:40,650
On the life of my only child,

956
00:53:40,700 --> 00:53:44,650
on my father's soul, I no longer bear him any grudge.

957
00:53:44,700 --> 00:53:47,750
From the bottom of my heart I wish he'd come to his senses.

958
00:53:47,800 --> 00:53:51,800
Well... then maybe we can do something for him.

959
00:53:52,800 --> 00:53:56,360
The Colonel wasn't home. I left word for him to come here when he's back.

960
00:53:56,400 --> 00:53:57,740
We need to consult him too.

961
00:53:57,780 --> 00:54:00,840
I think if we stage a scene where

962
00:54:00,880 --> 00:54:03,000
an Englishman negotiates with Sir

963
00:54:03,040 --> 00:54:04,280
and promises him

964
00:54:04,320 --> 00:54:06,480
the English have forgiven his sins,

965
00:54:06,520 --> 00:54:08,655
maybe his state of mind will change.

966
00:54:08,922 --> 00:54:10,680
Even if Churchill himself came

967
00:54:10,720 --> 00:54:12,300
and signed a notarized deed,

968
00:54:12,340 --> 00:54:14,840
I don't think it would get into Sir's head

969
00:54:14,880 --> 00:54:17,380
that the English have given up their hostility to him.

970
00:54:17,420 --> 00:54:19,460
I mean, he doesn't want to believe it.

971
00:54:19,500 --> 00:54:22,720
Suppose a man really had fought the English,

972
00:54:22,760 --> 00:54:24,960
cut their soldiers down in blood,

973
00:54:25,000 --> 00:54:27,520
and wrecked their colonial plans.

974
00:54:27,560 --> 00:54:30,260
Do you think a man like that would believe

975
00:54:30,300 --> 00:54:32,640
the English would one day forgive his sins?

976
00:54:32,680 --> 00:54:36,320
If a third enemy is involved, it's possible.

977
00:54:37,322 --> 00:54:41,322
It's happened many times that, to crush a third enemy, two enemies

978
00:54:41,482 --> 00:54:43,480
declared a truce.

979
00:54:43,520 --> 00:54:45,100
The way I see this Sir of ours,

980
00:54:45,140 --> 00:54:47,700
he's decided his own fate.

981
00:54:47,740 --> 00:54:49,580
The English must torment him.

982
00:54:49,620 --> 00:54:52,020
In the end they must exile him like Napoleon.

983
00:54:52,060 --> 00:54:53,940
I promise you he'll be like

984
00:54:53,980 --> 00:54:55,660
a passenger on a ship

985
00:54:55,700 --> 00:54:57,840
gazing at the hills of St. Helena

986
00:54:57,880 --> 00:54:59,300
from afar.

987
00:54:59,340 --> 00:55:01,460
Where do we find this English envoy?

988
00:55:01,500 --> 00:55:04,520
The Indian, Sardar Maharat-Khan, came back from the south today.

989
00:55:04,560 --> 00:55:06,200
I somehow talked him into

990
00:55:06,240 --> 00:55:08,260
finding us an English representative.

991
00:55:08,300 --> 00:55:09,680
We need the Colonel.

992
00:55:09,720 --> 00:55:13,090
I won't hold back from anything I can do,

993
00:55:14,730 --> 00:55:18,035
but I have no faith at all in this plan of yours.

994
00:55:18,870 --> 00:55:19,580
Uncle Asdollah,

995
00:55:19,620 --> 00:55:21,380
did the master builder tell you

996
00:55:21,420 --> 00:55:23,815
Sardar Maharat-Khan is back?

997
00:55:23,930 --> 00:55:24,740
You little devil!

998
00:55:24,780 --> 00:55:26,660
Now you're making up stories about me?

999
00:55:26,700 --> 00:55:28,120
I swear I didn't mean anything...

1000
00:55:28,160 --> 00:55:29,760
"Just," my foot!

1001
00:55:29,800 --> 00:55:31,940
Sardar Maharat-Khan is a close friend of mine.

1002
00:55:31,980 --> 00:55:35,180
He does business with the English, that's why he goes south so much.

1003
00:55:35,220 --> 00:55:38,480
This morning I saw Sardar Maharat-Khan's wife at your house myself.

1004
00:55:38,520 --> 00:55:39,745
Moment!

1005
00:55:40,450 --> 00:55:42,740
When the Sardar travels, he leaves his wife in my care.

1006
00:55:42,780 --> 00:55:46,080
How could I let her stay home alone?

1007
00:55:46,120 --> 00:55:49,000
With a married woman, I'd never dream of San Francisco.

1008
00:55:49,040 --> 00:55:49,740
Never!

1009
00:55:49,780 --> 00:55:51,640
Do you understand what I'm saying or not?

1010
00:55:51,680 --> 00:55:53,860
Such slander doesn't stick to me.

1011
00:55:53,900 --> 00:55:56,390
Uncle Asdollah, don't you remember lesson seventeen?

1012
00:55:56,440 --> 00:55:58,930
You said, when a trip to San Francisco comes up,

1013
00:55:58,980 --> 00:56:02,025
first hit the road, then see who your travel companion is.

1014
00:56:04,200 --> 00:56:05,970
What a cheeky kid!

1015
00:56:06,020 --> 00:56:09,585
So I said something, and you have to believe it?

1016
00:56:10,520 --> 00:56:12,390
All your strength went into your jaw?

1017
00:56:12,440 --> 00:56:15,010
As the Sardar says, "Your nature is very droopy, hey!

1018
00:56:15,060 --> 00:56:17,520
Your jaw is very strong, hey!"

1019
00:56:17,780 --> 00:56:19,240
What a nosy kid!

1020
00:56:19,280 --> 00:56:24,740
So, Uncle Asdollah, do you think this plan for Dear Uncle will work?

1021
00:56:24,780 --> 00:56:26,540
Pray it works.

1022
00:56:26,580 --> 00:56:30,000
You and your Agha Jan caused this whole mess.

1023
00:56:30,050 --> 00:56:34,980
Called a low-born provincial, your Agha Jan set about driving the old man nuts.

1024
00:56:35,020 --> 00:56:40,020
And you, too gutless for San Francisco, set off firecrackers and drove him mad.

1025
00:56:40,730 --> 00:56:42,050
What a father and son! A perfect match.

1026
00:56:42,100 --> 00:56:45,050
Yes, yes, you weren't here today.

1027
00:56:45,100 --> 00:56:47,570
You should have seen how narrowly Sir escaped disaster.

1028
00:56:47,620 --> 00:56:50,090
No, sir, I won't let the dignity of Brother,

1029
00:56:50,140 --> 00:56:51,690
who is the head of our family,

1030
00:56:51,740 --> 00:56:54,130
become a plaything for all and sundry.

1031
00:56:54,180 --> 00:56:56,050
Unfortunately, there's no other way.

1032
00:56:56,100 --> 00:56:59,330
Either Sir is committed to a mental hospital,

1033
00:56:59,380 --> 00:57:00,010
or else...

1034
00:57:00,060 --> 00:57:01,580
Don't talk nonsense, Asdollah.

1035
00:57:01,620 --> 00:57:04,060
I'd put a bullet in my own brain

1036
00:57:04,100 --> 00:57:07,410
before I'd let Brother be taken to an asylum.

1037
00:57:07,460 --> 00:57:10,690
The century-old good name of a noble family is no joke.

1038
00:57:10,740 --> 00:57:12,485
Hello, Uncle.

1039
00:57:12,820 --> 00:57:15,290
Why bring up these things in front of the kids?

1040
00:57:15,340 --> 00:57:17,900
Moment, Mr. Colonel!

1041
00:57:17,980 --> 00:57:19,970
First, this boy isn't a kid anymore.

1042
00:57:20,020 --> 00:57:21,690
He's a sensible young man.

1043
00:57:21,740 --> 00:57:24,300
Second, if disaster was averted today, it was thanks to him.

1044
00:57:24,340 --> 00:57:25,760
Please.

1045
00:57:25,830 --> 00:57:28,860
Why can't you see how serious this is?

1046
00:57:28,900 --> 00:57:31,360
Today Sir nearly killed Mash Ghasem.

1047
00:57:31,400 --> 00:57:33,360
And if he had, he'd be in prison now.

1048
00:57:33,400 --> 00:57:34,460
We must act soon.

1049
00:57:34,500 --> 00:57:37,870
Do you know what a scandal there'd have been then?

1050
00:57:41,230 --> 00:57:44,780
I'm ready to give my life for Brother's health,

1051
00:57:44,830 --> 00:57:47,875
but only if we think more sensibly.

1052
00:57:48,240 --> 00:57:51,520
But I don't think Brother will believe

1053
00:57:51,560 --> 00:57:54,280
that the English forgave his sins that easily.

1054
00:57:54,320 --> 00:57:56,910
Of course, if we find the right man,

1055
00:57:57,270 --> 00:57:59,740
we've weighed every angle carefully.

1056
00:57:59,780 --> 00:58:02,900
That representative will first propose harsh terms,

1057
00:58:02,940 --> 00:58:05,180
then, through our mediation, he'll soften,

1058
00:58:05,220 --> 00:58:06,940
and in the end he'll agree.

1059
00:58:06,980 --> 00:58:10,180
If Sir doesn't obstruct English affairs,

1060
00:58:10,220 --> 00:58:13,640
he'll send a favorable report up to his superiors.

1061
00:58:13,690 --> 00:58:17,690
On what pretext will you raise this with Brother?

1062
00:58:17,790 --> 00:58:20,900
Just say the English suddenly felt like contacting him?

1063
00:58:20,940 --> 00:58:24,900
No, we'll say the English are in a bad way in the war with Germany,

1064
00:58:24,940 --> 00:58:28,940
so now they've decided to make peace with local opponents all over the world.

1065
00:58:29,670 --> 00:58:30,900
Leave Sir to me.

1066
00:58:30,940 --> 00:58:35,900
I talked at length with the Sardar today. The poor man means very well,

1067
00:58:35,940 --> 00:58:38,620
but he said he can't find an Englishman.

1068
00:58:38,660 --> 00:58:41,140
Then things aren't settled yet.

1069
00:58:41,180 --> 00:58:43,820
Oh, they are! He said he has an Indian friend

1070
00:58:43,860 --> 00:58:46,385
who's a corporal in the Allied army.

1071
00:58:47,370 --> 00:58:49,760
The Sardar said he can talk him into

1072
00:58:49,800 --> 00:58:52,040
taking part in our little play.

1073
00:58:52,080 --> 00:58:54,360
In exchange for some reward, of course.

1074
00:58:54,400 --> 00:58:58,940
I doubt Sir would agree to negotiate with an Indian corporal.

1075
00:58:59,810 --> 00:59:01,500
What does this Indian look like?

1076
00:59:01,540 --> 00:59:03,740
Can he be passed off as an Englishman?

1077
00:59:03,780 --> 00:59:07,300
Forget an Englishman, he couldn't even pass for a Baluchi.

1078
00:59:07,340 --> 00:59:12,120
From what the Sardar said, he's the color of strong milky coffee, head to toe.

1079
00:59:14,090 --> 00:59:16,430
I think we need another idea.

1080
00:59:16,480 --> 00:59:20,540
Besides, even if you get Brother to negotiate with an Indian,

1081
00:59:20,580 --> 00:59:22,820
what about his rank?

1082
00:59:22,860 --> 00:59:25,760
Brother won't accept anyone below a general.

1083
00:59:25,800 --> 00:59:27,640
Rank doesn't matter.

1084
00:59:27,680 --> 00:59:31,640
He doesn't know British army ranks. We'll say he's a colonel.

1085
00:59:31,680 --> 00:59:35,560
Have you even spoken to Brother himself yet?

1086
00:59:35,600 --> 00:59:37,500
I've prepared his mind a little.

1087
00:59:37,540 --> 00:59:42,240
Last time I saw him, I made up that everywhere, occupied or not, the English

1088
00:59:42,280 --> 00:59:46,120
are scrambling to win their opponents over.

1089
00:59:46,170 --> 00:59:47,360
How did he react?

1090
00:59:47,400 --> 00:59:50,420
He blustered plenty, that if the English contact him

1091
00:59:50,460 --> 00:59:52,640
he'll never give in, and so on.

1092
00:59:52,680 --> 00:59:56,100
But I think once we carry out our plan, he'll agree.

1093
00:59:56,140 --> 00:59:58,700
He said he doesn't trust the English at all,

1094
00:59:58,740 --> 01:00:01,100
and if the English are to send him a representative,

1095
01:00:01,140 --> 01:00:03,520
he'll first order him disarmed.

1096
01:00:03,560 --> 01:00:06,560
He said he'll hide Mash Ghasem behind the curtain with a rifle,

1097
01:00:06,600 --> 01:00:08,900
so if the English envoy tries to double-cross him,

1098
01:00:08,940 --> 01:00:11,740
Mash Ghasem can shoot first and finish him off.

1099
01:00:11,780 --> 01:00:14,460
I'm afraid this will land us all in trouble.

1100
01:00:14,500 --> 01:00:19,000
Say this Indian reaches into his pocket for a hanky to blow his nose...

1101
01:00:19,610 --> 01:00:23,565
Heaven help us the day Sir tells Mash Ghasem to fire.

1102
01:00:23,890 --> 01:00:27,400
Then do you know what the English will do to us?

1103
01:00:27,450 --> 01:00:31,450
You're right. I think we should let Mash Ghasem in on it too.

1104
01:00:31,610 --> 01:00:32,710
Mash Ghasem's in the garden.

1105
01:00:32,750 --> 01:00:33,760
Shall I go call him?

1106
01:00:33,800 --> 01:00:34,190
Not a bad idea.

1107
01:00:34,230 --> 01:00:34,960
No, no.

1108
01:00:35,010 --> 01:00:36,235
Moment!

1109
01:00:36,870 --> 01:00:38,680
He mustn't know we held a meeting.

1110
01:00:38,730 --> 01:00:40,180
Better if we go to him.

1111
01:00:40,230 --> 01:00:41,960
So it looks like a coincidence.

1112
01:00:42,010 --> 01:00:42,960
Come.

1113
01:00:43,010 --> 01:00:45,015
After you, Colonel.

